<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-01">
<title>Module 1: Vectors</title>

<subsection xml:id="learning-outcomes">
<title>Learning Outcomes</title>

<p>After completing this module, you should be able to:</p>

<ul>
    <li>Perform basic operations on vectors in <m>\mathbb R^n</m>.</li>
    <li>Interpret vectors geometrically in <m>\mathbb R^2</m> and <m>\mathbb R^3</m>.</li>
    <li>Convert between algebraic and geometric representations.</li>
    <li>Compute angles between vectors and test orthogonality.</li>
    <li>Compute orthogonal projections and rejections.</li>
    <li>Apply projections to distance problems.</li>
</ul>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="vectors">
<title>Vectors</title>

<subsubsection xml:id="components-length">
<title>Components and Length</title>

<p>
Let
<m>\mathbf v=(v_1,v_2,\ldots,v_n)^T\in\mathbb R^n</m>.
The norm (or length) of <m>\mathbf v</m> is
</p>

<me>
\|\mathbf v\|
=
\sqrt{v_1^2+v_2^2+\cdots+v_n^2}.
</me>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="unit-vectors">
<title>Unit Vectors</title>

<p>
If <m>\mathbf v\neq \mathbf 0</m>, then the unit vector in the
direction of <m>\mathbf v</m> is
</p>

<me>
\widehat{\mathbf v}
=
\frac{\mathbf v}{\|\mathbf v\|}.
</me>

<example>
<title>Computing a Unit Vector</title>

<me>
\mathbf v=
\begin{bmatrix}
3\\4
\end{bmatrix}
</me>

<me>
\|\mathbf v\|
=
5
</me>

<me>
\widehat{\mathbf v}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
3/5\\
4/5
\end{bmatrix}.
</me>

</example>

</subsubsection>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="vector-operations">
<title>Basic Vector Operations</title>

<subsubsection xml:id="vector-addition">
<title>Vector Addition</title>

<p>
Vector addition is performed componentwise.
Geometrically, vector addition follows the parallelogram rule.
</p>
 TBD
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="scalar-multiplication">
<title>Scalar Multiplication</title>

<p>
For a scalar <m>\alpha</m>,
</p>

<me>
\alpha\mathbf u
=
(\alpha u_1,\ldots,\alpha u_n).
</me>

<p>
Scaling changes a vector's length. Negative scalars reverse direction.
</p>

</subsubsection>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="dot-product">
<title>Dot Product and Orthogonality</title>

<subsubsection xml:id="dot-product-definition">
<title>Dot Product</title>

<me>
\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v
=
\sum_{i=1}^{n}u_iv_i.
</me>

<example>
<title>Dot Product Example</title>

<me>
\mathbf u=
\begin{bmatrix}
1\\2\\0\\1
\end{bmatrix},
\qquad
\mathbf v=
\begin{bmatrix}
0\\1\\-1\\1
\end{bmatrix}
</me>

<me>
\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v
=
3.
</me>

</example>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="angle-between-vectors">
<title>Angle Between Vectors</title>

<theorem>
<title>Angle Formula</title>

<statement>
<me>
\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v
=
\|\mathbf u\|
\|\mathbf v\|
\cos(\theta).
</me>

Hence,

<me>
\theta
=
\arccos
\left(
\frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v}
{\|\mathbf u\|\|\mathbf v\|}
\right).
</me>
</statement>

</theorem>
TBD

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="orthogonality">
<title>Orthogonality</title>

<p>
Vectors are orthogonal if and only if
</p>

<me>
\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v=0.
</me>
tbd
</subsubsection>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="projection">
<title>Orthogonal Projection</title>

<subsubsection>
<title>Projection Formula</title>

<me>
\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf d}(\mathbf u)
=
\frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf d}
{\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf d}
\mathbf d.
</me>

<me>
\operatorname{oproj}_{\mathbf d}(\mathbf u)
=
\mathbf u
-
\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf d}(\mathbf u).
</me>

</subsubsection>

<subsubsection>
<title>Interactive Projection</title>

tbd

</subsubsection>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="distance-point-line">
<title>Distance from a Point to a Line</title>

<p>
Consider a line
</p>

<me>
L=
\{\mathbf A+t\mathbf d:t\in\mathbb R\}.
</me>

<p>
Let
<m>\mathbf{AB}=\mathbf B-\mathbf A</m>.
The shortest distance from <m>B</m> to the line is
</p>

<me>
\operatorname{dist}(B,L)
=
\left\|
\mathbf{AB}
-
\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf d}
(\mathbf{AB})
\right\|.
</me>
tbd

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="quick-reference">
<title>Quick Reference</title>

<md>
\|\mathbf v\|
=
\sqrt{\sum_i v_i^2}
</md>

<md>
\widehat{\mathbf v}
=
\frac{\mathbf v}{\|\mathbf v\|}
</md>

<md>
\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v
=
\sum_i u_i v_i
</md>

<md>
\theta
=
\arccos
\left(
\frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v}
{\|\mathbf u\|\|\mathbf v\|}
\right)
</md>

<md>
\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf d}(\mathbf u)
=
\frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf d}
{\|\mathbf d\|^2}\mathbf d
</md>

<md>
\operatorname{oproj}_{\mathbf d}(\mathbf u)
=
\mathbf u-\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf d}(\mathbf u)
</md>

</subsection>

</section>
